1
00:00:03,150 --> 00:00:04,900
Welcome aboard.

2
00:00:05,150 --> 00:00:06,900
I’m glad you’re here.

3
00:00:07,150 --> 00:00:16,425
Over the next fifteen lessons, we’re going to work through the Navigation Rules together, the part of the captain’s exam that trips up more people than any other.

4
00:00:16,675 --> 00:00:18,225
Here’s why.

5
00:00:18,475 --> 00:00:21,875
Every other module needs seventy percent to pass.

6
00:00:22,125 --> 00:00:24,075
This one needs ninety.

7
00:00:24,325 --> 00:00:28,025
Out of fifty questions, you can miss only about five.

8
00:00:28,275 --> 00:00:37,925
So we’ll cover all thirty-seven rules, plus the annexes the exam pulls from, and after each lesson there’s a short check so you can make sure it stuck.

9
00:00:38,925 --> 00:00:42,125
Don’t worry about memorizing everything today.

10
00:00:42,375 --> 00:00:45,425
Just get comfortable with how the rules are built.

11
00:00:45,675 --> 00:00:49,850
The memorizing comes later, and I’ll give you plenty of tricks for it.

12
00:00:51,300 --> 00:00:53,325
Let’s start with a picture.

13
00:00:53,575 --> 00:00:59,325
It’s five-thirty in the morning, and you’re leaving the dock in Tampa Bay with four anglers aboard.

14
00:00:59,575 --> 00:01:05,325
You run down the bay, out past Egmont Key, and into the open Gulf to fish a ledge.

15
00:01:05,575 --> 00:01:07,450
Here’s my question.

16
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:11,425
Somewhere on that run, the rules you were following changed.

17
00:01:11,675 --> 00:01:13,325
Did you notice?

18
00:01:14,325 --> 00:01:18,325
Most captains never do, because the differences are small.

19
00:01:18,575 --> 00:01:21,525
But they’re real, and the exam loves them.

20
00:01:21,775 --> 00:01:24,725
In the bay, you were under the Inland rules.

21
00:01:24,975 --> 00:01:28,550
Out in the Gulf, you were under the International rules.

22
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:30,750
So let’s find that line.

23
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:35,800
There are two rule books, and they’re mostly identical.

24
00:01:36,050 --> 00:01:42,875
The International rules, usually called the COLREGS, govern the high seas and the waters connected to them.

25
00:01:43,125 --> 00:01:53,025
They come from a 1972 treaty, while the Inland rules are American law, covering our harbors, bays, rivers and the Intracoastal Waterway.

26
00:01:53,275 --> 00:01:58,375
Each set applies to every vessel on its waters, whatever flag she flies.

27
00:01:59,375 --> 00:02:01,150
So where’s the line?

28
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:11,050
It’s called the COLREGS demarcation line, and it’s printed on your chart as a dashed magenta line, usually stretched across the mouth of a harbor or inlet.

29
00:02:11,300 --> 00:02:15,050
In Florida, you cross it every time you head out an inlet.

30
00:02:15,300 --> 00:02:19,000
Here’s some homework: find it on your local chart this week.

31
00:02:19,250 --> 00:02:21,225
It makes all of this real.

32
00:02:22,675 --> 00:02:28,425
Rule one also tells you what can sit alongside these rules, and there are four things to know.

33
00:02:28,675 --> 00:02:34,650
First, local authorities can make special rules for a particular harbor, river or lake.

34
00:02:34,900 --> 00:02:39,975
But those rules must conform as closely as possible to the Navigation Rules.

35
00:02:40,225 --> 00:02:43,000
They can add to them, not throw them out.

36
00:02:43,250 --> 00:02:51,700
Second, governments can allow extra lights, shapes or whistle signals for warships, convoys, or fishing fleets working together.

37
00:02:51,950 --> 00:02:55,525
So if you see an odd light on a Navy ship, that’s why.

38
00:02:55,775 --> 00:03:02,200
Third, traffic separation schemes, the highway lanes for big ships, are adopted internationally.

39
00:03:02,450 --> 00:03:04,850
We’ll cover them in lesson four.

40
00:03:05,100 --> 00:03:15,525
And fourth, a vessel of unusual construction that can’t place her lights exactly as required may use the closest alternative her government approves.

41
00:03:15,775 --> 00:03:19,900
Inland, that’s called a certificate of alternative compliance.

42
00:03:21,350 --> 00:03:23,125
Now, Rule two.

43
00:03:23,375 --> 00:03:27,800
If you remember only one rule from this whole course, make it this one.

44
00:03:28,050 --> 00:03:35,275
Rule two says nothing in the rules excuses you from the consequences of neglecting ordinary seamanship.

45
00:03:35,525 --> 00:03:39,025
In plain English: the rule book is not a shield.

46
00:03:39,275 --> 00:03:46,875
You must pay attention to every danger and every special circumstance, including what the other boat can and can’t do.

47
00:03:47,125 --> 00:03:52,325
And sometimes, to avoid immediate danger, you have to depart from the rules.

48
00:03:52,575 --> 00:03:55,400
That isn’t just allowed, it’s expected.

49
00:03:56,400 --> 00:03:58,425
Here’s how I think about it.

50
00:03:58,675 --> 00:04:05,425
Picture yourself as the stand-on boat, holding your course, while a tug pushing a barge clearly isn’t turning.

51
00:04:05,675 --> 00:04:11,700
If you hold your course right into that barge, you followed the rules, and you’ll still be found at fault.

52
00:04:11,950 --> 00:04:15,950
Notice too that the rules almost never say right of way.

53
00:04:16,200 --> 00:04:19,750
They say who keeps out of the way, and who stands on.

54
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:22,600
That’s a mindset, not a privilege.

55
00:04:24,050 --> 00:04:28,800
The rules end with annexes, and yes, the exam asks about them.

56
00:04:29,050 --> 00:04:31,850
Let me give you a one-line handle on each.

57
00:04:32,100 --> 00:04:38,350
Annex one is the engineering annex: exactly where lights go, how far apart, how bright.

58
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:45,100
Annex two covers extra signals for fishing boats working close together, like trawlers in a fleet.

59
00:04:45,350 --> 00:04:49,925
Annex three is the technical spec for whistles, bells and gongs.

60
00:04:50,175 --> 00:04:53,800
Annex four is the big one for you: distress signals.

61
00:04:54,050 --> 00:04:57,475
We’ll spend real time on those in lesson fourteen.

62
00:04:57,725 --> 00:05:07,325
And the Inland rules add Annex five, the pilot rules, which cover things like the blue lights on law enforcement boats and special anchorage areas.

63
00:05:08,775 --> 00:05:14,425
Before we move on, here are four habits that will save you points on every rules question.

64
00:05:14,675 --> 00:05:21,825
First, find the word Inland, International or Both at the start of the question, and answer for that rule book.

65
00:05:22,075 --> 00:05:24,200
Circle it if you’re on paper.

66
00:05:24,450 --> 00:05:26,700
Second, watch vessel lengths.

67
00:05:26,950 --> 00:05:34,075
Seven, twelve, twenty, fifty and one hundred meters are the magic numbers, and rules change at each one.

68
00:05:34,325 --> 00:05:39,300
Third, ask yourself: are these boats in sight of each other, or are we in fog?

69
00:05:39,550 --> 00:05:43,025
Different rules apply, and you’ll see why in lesson eight.

70
00:05:43,275 --> 00:05:49,975
And fourth, when two answers both sound reasonable, pick the one that matches the rule’s actual wording.

71
00:05:50,225 --> 00:05:55,325
The exam is written straight from the rule book, not from what people do at the dock.

72
00:05:56,325 --> 00:05:58,150
That’s lesson one.

73
00:05:58,400 --> 00:06:04,225
Next, we’ll nail down the definitions, the vocabulary every other rule depends on.
